# Unix Philosophy Rules

Follow these principles in all code:

- Modularity: small parts with clean interfaces.
- Clarity: clarity beats cleverness.
- Composition: design to connect with other programs.
- Separation: policy vs mechanism; interfaces vs engines.
- Simplicity: add complexity only when required.
- Parsimony: write big programs only when necessary.
- Transparency: make inspection and debugging easy.
- Robustness: transparency + simplicity produce robustness.
- Representation: put knowledge in data, keep logic simple.
- Least Surprise: do the least surprising thing.
- Silence: say nothing when nothing is surprising.
- Repair: fail loudly and early when failure is required.
- Economy: save programmer time over machine time.
- Generation: automate instead of hand-hacking.
- Optimization: prototype first, optimize later.
- Diversity: avoid "one true way".
- Extensibility: design for future change.

## Examples

```python
# BAD: hidden side effects, surprising behavior
def send_report(user, report):
    print("sending report")  # noisy in normal success path
    return smtp.send(user.email, report)

# GOOD: quiet success, loud failure, clear intent
def send_report(user, report):
    if not user.email:
        raise ValueError("user.email is required")
    smtp.send(user.email, report)
```

```python
# BAD: policy mixed with mechanism
def save_user(user):
    db.insert("users", user.to_dict())
    audit.log("users", user.id)
    if user.plan == "pro":
        notifier.send(user.email, "welcome")

# GOOD: policy separated from mechanism
def save_user(user):
    persist_user(user)
    run_user_policies(user)
```
